# V5 data-source feasibility audit — 2026-09-07

Scope: exact Nasdaq-100 and Russell 2000 membership from 2021-01-04 through 2025-12-31, plus causal industry classifications; subsequently extended by the controller to a read-only local S&P import/image-preparation review below. No keys, account access, tests, provider data downloads, source/data edits, purchases, or Docker actions. This report is the only file written. The controller's observation that four FMP constituent endpoints returned HTTP 402 is accepted as session evidence, not independently reproduced. Subsequent controller diagnosis confirmed FMP profile HTTP 200 with a valid key, historical Nasdaq HTTP 402 with restricted/subscription/upgrade wording, and Alpaca SIP 2020 daily HTTP 200: this distinguishes the constituent entitlement gap from an invalid key or blanket historical-price access failure.

## Decision

No complete, authenticated daily dataset covering both requested indexes and causal industry is established by this audit. Nasdaq public reconstruction is a concrete feasible research path, but needs initial-state and event reconciliation. Exact Russell 2000 remains the main membership sourcing blocker with the currently authorized Alpaca/FMP accounts. SEC filings offer a promising public causal **SIC** source, subject to taxonomy acceptance and complete identifier/coverage validation. Norgate Platinum offers strong membership coverage but is neither a verified complete V5 solution nor a perpetual one-time export license.

## Existing accounts and public membership sources

- **Alpaca:** the documented `/v2/assets` list describes available securities and exchange/tradability filtering. Nasdaq-listed assets are not Nasdaq-100 members. No exact historical constituent or historical industry endpoint was established from its public documentation. [Alpaca assets guide](https://docs.alpaca.markets/us/docs/working-with-assets)
- **FMP:** its documented `/stable/historical-nasdaq-constituent` provides dated additions/removals. This is a relevant route if entitlement becomes available, but the controller observed HTTP 402. Documentation does not by itself certify complete daily membership, historical announcement timestamps, or causal industry. Current company-profile industry labels must not be backfilled across 2021–2025. No documented exact Russell 2000 constituent history endpoint was found. [Historical Nasdaq documentation](https://site.financialmodelingprep.com/developer/docs/stable/historical-nasdaq), [profile documentation](https://site.financialmodelingprep.com/developer/docs/stable/profile-symbol)
- **Nasdaq primary announcements:** accessible announcements supply additions, removals, publication dates, and effective-before-open dates. Concrete examples include the [December 2020 reconstitution](https://www.nasdaq.com/press-release/annual-changes-to-the-nasdaq-100-index-2020-12-11), [2021 reconstitution](https://www.nasdaq.com/press-release/annual-changes-to-the-nasdaq-100-indexr-2021-12-10), and [CrowdStrike replacing Maxim effective August 26, 2021](https://ir.nasdaq.com/news-releases/news-release-details/crowdstrike-holdings-inc-join-nasdaq-100-index-beginning-august). Annual notices alone are insufficient: incorporate interim changes and superseding notices, including the [2023 reconstitution update for TTWO/SGEN](https://ir.nasdaq.com/node/106911/pdf).
- **Immutable Wikipedia discovery anchors:** the component tables have moved out of the main Nasdaq-100 article. Observed permanent revisions: [historical changes, oldid 1371960136](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Historical_components_of_the_Nasdaq-100&oldid=1371960136) and [component list, oldid 1368915166](https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_NASDAQ-100_companies&oldid=1368915166). These are secondary discovery/cross-check sources, not proof every historical state is correct or was known then. The audit did not recover and validate the complete January 4, 2021 opening set.
- **Concrete Nasdaq reconciliation blocker:** that frozen historical page gives SOLS entry October 30, 2025 and exit November 6, while [Nasdaq's own 2025 retrospective](https://www.nasdaq.com/articles/global-indexes/2025-nasdaq-100-reconstitution-and-performance-highlights) gives October 20 and November 10. Resolve against contemporaneous index notices; neither silently select a date nor certify the reconstruction meanwhile. Preserve separate announcement, effective, and retrieval timestamps. The public [GIW weighting page](https://indexes.nasdaqomx.com/index/Weighting/NDX) explicitly requests login for full access; no attempt was made to bypass that boundary.
- **Russell 2000:** LSEG's [public constituent spreadsheets](https://www.lseg.com/en/ftse-russell/index-resources/constituent-weights?indexName=RU2000) are selected-index snapshots, typically published with a delay. No complete public 2021–2025 daily history was located. The legacy 2021 membership-PDF URL failed retrieval in the browsing tool; that does not prove it is globally unavailable. Even an annual PDF series cannot resolve quarterly IPO additions and daily corporate actions documented by [FTSE Russell](https://www.lseg.com/content/dam/ftse-russell/en_us/documents/other/russell-2000-index-product-highlights-revisions-cut-sheet.pdf). IWM holdings and a market-cap approximation are unsuitable replacements. [Algoseek's primary documentation](https://algoseek.com/docs/rest-api/reference/endpoint-v-1-data-us-equity-index-index-comp-get) advertises point-in-time Russell and Nasdaq constituent records covering these years, but entitlement, exact delivery fields, completeness, licensing, and price were not verified; it is only another vendor lead.

## Public causal industry option

The [SEC Financial Statement Data Sets documentation](https://www.sec.gov/files/financial-statement-data-sets.pdf) defines `SUB.sic` as the SEC industry assignment as of the filing date, and supplies CIK, accession, filing date, and acceptance timestamp. The [public quarterly archive](https://www.sec.gov/data-research/sec-markets-data/financial-statement-data-sets) spans the required period and earlier seed years.

Feasible construction: use a declared, frozen SIC taxonomy; bind each symbol/share class to the correct historical issuer; select the latest qualifying filing available by the decision cutoff; use earlier filings to seed January 2021; resolve missing/zero SIC and foreign-filer gaps through the original filings. Preserve accession, source bytes/hash, acceptance timestamp and timestamp semantics. Do not use fiscal period-end as availability or propagate a later classification backward. This is causal SIC, not GICS/ICB/TRBC; a taxonomy substitution needs explicit acceptance. Full universe coverage has not been checked. SEC notes some archives were reprocessed, so original filing provenance remains valuable. A licensed historical TRBC alternative is documented by [LSEG](https://www.lseg.com/content/dam/data-analytics/en_us/documents/brochures/lseg-data-for-quant-research-brochure.pdf), but this user has no such entitlement.

## Norgate Platinum: independent assessment

- **Coverage and price:** US Platinum advertises delisted securities and historical index constituents; current public price is **USD 346.50 for six months or USD 630 for twelve months**. Its tables list Nasdaq-100 membership from October 1993 and Russell 2000 from July 1990. [Package pricing](https://norgatedata.com/stockmarketpackages.php), [coverage tables](https://norgatedata.com/data-content-tables.php)
- **Python extraction:** vendor-maintained documentation supports `index_constituent_timeseries`, NumPy/Pandas output, current-and-past security lists, and stable asset IDs. A practical authorized extraction would enumerate all current/past members, including delisted securities, and collect date-bounded membership for `$NDX` and `$RUT`; freeze exports with acquisition/version metadata and hashes. The package requires an active subscription and running Windows Norgate Data Updater. Its `classification`/`classification_at_level` interface returns a single value and exposes no as-of date or historical series. Therefore its public API does **not establish causal historical industry**. [Python documentation](https://pypi.org/project/norgatedata/)
- **Exactness limits:** Norgate says membership is daily/effective-date based and incorporates interim changes, but expressly excludes temporary inclusions and supplies no announcement dates. Its corrections are applied without notification or versioning. These matter to complete membership, information availability, and reproducible byte-pinned V5 inputs; a raw export cannot be assumed sufficient without supplemental evidence and coverage reconciliation. [Data FAQ](https://norgatedata.com/data-package-faq.php)
- **License/retention:** published terms allow personal use by one individual, normally on two computers, and prohibit redistribution and other commercial use. The licensing FAQ says extracted/exported data, metadata, and backups must be deleted after expiry when renewal is not imminent; it says permanent-retention exceptions are unavailable. Derived trading results/rules may be retained, but an exact constituent/source bundle remains underlying data. A subscription must remain active for retained raw-data use. No purchase, account creation, or support contact occurred. [EULA dated 20260825](https://norgatedata.com/subscribe/eula.php), [subscription/licensing FAQ](https://norgatedata.com/faq.php)

## Actionable next steps

1. Continue the authorized S&P local work independently. Keep full three-index certification blocked until missing inputs are actually obtained and validated.
2. For Nasdaq, inventory and freeze the initial snapshot plus all official dated changes, amendments, ticker changes, and temporary lines. Resolve the concrete SOLS disagreement before publishing membership authority.
3. Evaluate SEC SIC against the campaign's accepted industry contract and measure historical identifier/coverage gaps before creating an industry authority.
4. For Russell, obtain a licensed exact history with its scope stated, or a demonstrably complete public initial-state/event archive. Norgate is a plausible membership acquisition route if the user authorizes a subscription, but industry, temporary-line coverage, availability evidence, and continuing retention costs remain separate requirements.

Evidence limits: this was bounded public-source research, not an exhaustive proof that no public source exists. No dataset completeness, byte-level export, real capture, or account entitlement was exercised.

## Follow-up: independent local import and image-preparation review

Verdict: **Approved for these completed preparation steps; no new Critical or Important finding.** This does not certify complete three-index inputs, historical industry coverage, or an executable baseline authority.

**S&P pair and helper.** Read `import-local-v5-sources.py` and the narrow normalization/validation helpers it invokes. The importer verifies original membership/HTML/spot-check/name bytes against the original provenance, checks the reviewed mapping records, requires replay to reproduce the original membership and spot-check bytes, then requires the new export to equal only the seed-date transformation before publishing (`import-local-v5-sources.py:43,49,52,54,56,58`). The normalizer rejects inconsistent forward/reverse transitions (`core/public_membership.py:398,407,416`). Pair validation checks bytes, row count, symbol count and boundary dates (`normalize_pit_universe_membership.py:308`). The provenance explicitly retains the original revision/acquisition identity and denies full provider certification (`import-local-v5-sources.py:70,82,87`).

Independent standard-library, read-only byte checks confirmed:

- All five copied S&P source/map files match the original recorded hashes; copied provenance, helper, and normalizer match their recorded hashes; reviewed mapping records remain identical.
- The V5 CSV is byte-identical to the original CSV after replacing the January 1 seed dates with January 4. It has 711 events, 606 unique tickers, and 505 initial member rows. First/last event dates are January 4, 2021 and December 22, 2025. These are security membership counts, not an assertion of exactly 500 share classes.
- Membership SHA-256 is `01f3cdda35856027be26551171f2d1c687844a2e323a3471a6c3ae6ad3eda40a` and matches the V5 provenance.

The original HTML normalization and official spot-check execution were assessed from source, not rerun. The helper is one-shot acquisition scratch: failure can leave a partial create-only cache or CSV, and it is not restartable/transactional. The completed pair is present and consistent; an incomplete pair must remain unadmitted. No additional producer hardening is necessary to accept this already completed local import. The helper's SEC copy logic checks both streaming source and reread destination hashes before writing import provenance; the 2.97 GB archive hashing was not repeated. Those archives remain raw material and do not establish historical industry or CIK-to-security continuity.

**Image artifact chain.** Read the preparation report, `record-evaluator-image.py`, recipe/ignore file, runtime map, resource/profile leaves, and preparation index. Independently verified the index SHA-256 `65618a8bb60c50295f1d55468f4abc249f658782e5025fcc5c3ece71383c26df` and each of its four referenced artifact hashes. The runtime map has exactly the contract's 56 source paths. All 56 current checkout files and staged copies match their recorded raw-byte hashes; the staged context has exactly those files plus three recipe/lock files: 59 files and 1,408,017 bytes. Recipe hashes also match both locations. Image reference, runtime-source identity, and CPU/memory/PID/output resource bindings agree across the artifacts.

The recorded smoke source uses the immutable image reference, no host mounts, disabled networking, read-only root, non-root UID/GID, bounded resources, and installed-source verification before imports (`record-evaluator-image.py:23,33,38`). It does not call evaluator/probe entrypoints. I did not run Docker or independently repeat the smoke. The OCI platform/config digests are literals in the recording helper (`record-evaluator-image.py:55`); their relationship to actual OCI blobs was not independently established by this file-only review. Treat runtime execution evidence as the image worker's recorded observation, distinct from the independently verified local artifact/reference hashes.

The image report accurately retains two preparation limitations: worktree-root ignore behavior failed and must not be reused as an exact-source guarantee; the successful accepted context avoids that issue. Two Linux transitive dependencies were resolved outside the Windows lock, so fresh-build reproducibility still needs a Linux-complete lock; the obtained image is nevertheless content pinned. Neither limitation invalidates the verified exact context or local preparation chain.

Only an in-memory standard-library Python reader/hash checker was executed for this review (`py -3.13 -B -`); it imported no project modules, wrote no bytecode/artifacts, and ran no tests, evaluator, or provider operations. File references in this section are relative to this worktree, with the two scratch helpers in the campaign directory containing this report.

## Follow-up: SEC SIC, reference prices, cached material, and Nasdaq evidence

Verdict: **Approved as obtained preparation material; no new Critical or Important defect found. Final V5 admission remains blocked.** Read the four acquisition/sealing helpers without executing them. No tests, provider requests, Docker, evaluation, credential reads, or source/data modifications were performed.

**SEC SIC and availability.** The acquisition helper restricts retrieval to HTTPS SEC URLs, records raw receipt hashes and retrieval metadata, and retains each extracted SUB source (`acquire-sec-sic-sources.py:42,87`). The sealing helper checks downloaded and extracted hashes before composition, rejects conflicting accession/CIK duplicates, retains missing SIC, and uses the first supplied session strictly after the raw accepted calendar date (`seal-v5-preparation.py:38,47,54,71,79`). This is conservatively dated filing evidence, not a claim that current SIC can be backdated or that historical industry authority is complete.

Independent standard-library read-only checks verified the normalized CSV, its acquisition-manifest and helper digests; the exact 2019Q4 plus 2020–2025 quarter inventory; all 172,551 unique accession/CIK rows and their availability dates against the actual supplied SPY session list; 10,567 CIKs; all 1,951 unknown SIC rows; and all 21 rows beyond the supplied calendar cutoff. Five selected raw SUB records (boundary, middle, unknown-SIC, and after-cutoff examples) match the normalized accession, CIK, SIC, form, and verbatim accepted timestamp. The 2.6 GB source zip hashing was not repeated. Pre-2020 accepted records map to the first *supplied* session, January 2, 2020; the column explicitly states that bounded-calendar meaning. Complete security lineage, latest-filing coverage and causal group ranking remain necessary before industry admission.

**References and prior cache.** The acquisition helper uses the existing SIP split/RAW fetch and cutoff calibration paths, binds production helper hashes, and labels SPY/QQQ/IWM as non-tradable references (`acquire-v5-reference-prices.py:17,23,25,27,32`). Focused unchanged-code checks addressed the concrete risks of an incomplete reference calendar and invalid cutoff calibration: fetch composition requires each reference's exact expected sessions (`core/alpaca_pit_backfill.py:520,529`), while factor derivation uses shared tail dates and a stable suffix before application (`core/alpaca_pit_backfill.py:242,300`). All three local CSV hashes match provenance and are byte-identical for the obtained factor-1 result. Each reference has exactly the same 1,508 unique ordered sessions, January 2, 2020 through December 31, 2025; January 9, 2025 is excluded. Production-source hashes also match. This confirms the acquired reference material, not missing union-wide prices.

The cache importer checks source stability and original expected digests, then rehashes copied bytes (`import-v5-cache-material.py:14,30,35`). All 11 copied files independently match their recorded lengths and hashes: 54,052,860 bytes. Its provenance accurately distinguishes copied provenance roots from files bound by those roots and retains previous S&P-only scope, the old seed, and security exclusions. Those historical exclusions cannot be inherited as a complete V5 security master.

**Canonical profile and preparation record.** The canonical profile alias is created with the same authenticated profile digest and reauthenticated (`seal-v5-preparation.py:109`). Its obtained SHA-256 is `7a9c1e73046f7a00abcb5fc029350ea7e42e5841b179074a5ef43a94f5613483`. Independently verified acquisition-status SHA-256 `d34e0ea7501bdb2e64e495d1b34d25ee8c1c81c818262b24a4c60f15071e4b7c`, all ten local-evidence hashes, the image-index/profile hashes, and all twelve final-input existence states and present-file hashes. Exactly two final input files exist: the S&P source pair. The status correctly sets three-universe readiness false. Image runtime claims retain the file-only verification limit described above.

**Nasdaq diagnostic evidence.** Read `nasdaq-acquisition-audit.py`, the anchor extraction, correction ledger, and non-admitted reconciliation diagnostic. Independently verified all 16 successful raw receipt hashes and byte lengths. The extraction uses the January 3, 2021 frozen revision as its 102-security starting candidate. The ledger documents FISV/FI correction, amended TTWO/SGEN December 18, 2023 changes, ANSS July 28, 2025 removal, and the missing FB/META rename. Reconciliation of 91 corrected event claims produces no state errors and agrees with the reverse-derived 101-security end-state. That is a consistency check only: missing temporary additions/removals can cancel out. The diagnostic explicitly preserves unresolved SOLS dates and GRAL/GRALV transient coverage, and emits no membership source pair. That non-admission decision is correct; fund holdings or a matching endpoint cannot establish complete index history.

**Remaining blockers:** exact complete Russell membership; Nasdaq temporary-line and full event coverage; union-wide dated identifiers and no-exclusion price/fundamental coverage; and complete causal SIC lineage/group composition. No new obtained material resolves those by itself. Quote preparation for missing membership can proceed while retaining the existing Alpaca and SEC sources; this review grants no vendor-send or purchase authorization.
